WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:01:03.027 --> 00:01:15.016
Welcome to Arles, France — one of the richest Roman cities north of the Mediterranean, and a UNESCO World Heritage town since 1981.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:35.137
We are beginning our walk on Rue Jean Jaurès, heading toward the heart of the city, the Place de la République.

00:01:47.755 --> 00:01:59.742
This is the Place de la République, the main square of the old city and one of the finest public spaces in all of Provence.

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:22.796
At the center stands the Arles Obelisk, a 4th-century Roman monument carved from red granite quarried in Asia Minor.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:45.818
The obelisk originally stood in the center of the spina of the Roman circus, where chariots raced around it as a turning post.

00:02:55.525 --> 00:03:07.691
After the circus was abandoned in the 6th century, the obelisk fell and broke in two. It was rediscovered in 1389.

00:03:17.621 --> 00:03:29.610
It was re-erected here in 1676, in front of the new town hall, "for the greater glory of King Louis XIV" — the Sun King.

00:03:38.898 --> 00:03:50.898
The bronze lions at its base were added in 1829 by the sculptor Antoine Laurent Dantan, and the fountain pool was added by Henri Révoil in the 1860s.

00:04:01.318 --> 00:04:13.302
The square is framed by the Saint-Trophime Cathedral, the Hôtel de Ville, the chapel of Sainte-Anne, and the archbishop's palace.

00:04:24.824 --> 00:04:36.824
We are entering the Palais de l'Archevêché — the former Archbishop's Palace — which gives access to the Cloître Saint-Trophime.

00:04:46.313 --> 00:04:58.311
Arles' archbishop outranked all other bishops in Gaul as head of a primatial see, giving the city enormous religious and political influence throughout the early medieval period.

00:05:05.289 --> 00:05:17.277
Through here we'll reach the cloister of Saint-Trophime — one of the finest examples of Romanesque and Gothic architecture in Provence.

00:05:26.088 --> 00:05:38.086
Entry to the cloister is a separate ticket, typically around 6 euros, or included in the Arles monument pass.

00:05:48.339 --> 00:06:00.337
The cloister was built for the canons — priests who assisted the bishop and managed the cathedral — under the Augustinian rule.

00:06:10.844 --> 00:06:22.845
The south and west galleries of the cloister were built in the Gothic style of the late 14th century — note the pointed arches and ribbed vaults.

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:44.383
The north and east galleries, coming up, are Romanesque, built in the 12th and early 13th centuries, with rounded arches and barrel vaulting.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:07:03.973
The corner pillars are decorated with large carved figures of saints and apostles, considered among the masterpieces of Romanesque sculpture in southern France.

00:07:14.511 --> 00:07:26.508
The cloister is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, along with seven other Roman and Romanesque monuments of Arles, designated in 1981.

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:54.964
The warm golden limestone used throughout the cloister is the local stone of Provence, quarried from the hills of the Alpilles just south of the city.

00:08:04.801 --> 00:08:16.800
We are now going to go up to the roof top of the cloister and get a view of it from up above. 

00:08:48.512 --> 00:09:00.510
From up here on the rooftop terrace, you get a sweeping view over the cloister below, with the bell tower of Saint-Trophime rising dramatically against the blue sky.

00:09:08.752 --> 00:09:20.748
The bell tower dates from the 12th century and is one of the defining landmarks of the Arles skyline, visible from many parts of the old city.

00:09:26.778 --> 00:09:38.752
From up here, you can really appreciate the elegant rhythm of the arches and the warm, golden color of the local limestone.

00:09:49.141 --> 00:10:01.136
Walking around the rooftop, you can look down into the open courtyard — a tranquil garden that has changed very little over the past 800 years.

00:10:11.167 --> 00:10:23.333
The walkway we're on now runs along the top of the cloister and was originally used by the canons during processions and meditations.

00:10:33.532 --> 00:10:45.532
Arles has been a bishopric since the 3rd century and remained one of the most important religious centers in all of Gaul.

00:10:56.913 --> 00:11:09.079
The city's bishopric was held by a series of outstanding clerics, beginning with Saint Trophimus and continuing with Saint Honoratus and Saint Hilarius in the 5th century.

00:11:20.294 --> 00:11:32.292
The cloister is next to the cathedral Church of St. Trophime which we will tour next. 

00:11:55.874 --> 00:12:08.040
Walking back down through the interior — the carved capitals above each column are extraordinary, each one depicting biblical figures with remarkable craftsmanship.

00:12:35.421 --> 00:12:47.407
The cloister was sold as national property during the French Revolution and used as a warehouse before being restored and opened to the public in the 19th century.

00:12:52.830 --> 00:13:06.351
The capitals of the columns are extraordinary — each one depicts biblical scenes with remarkable expressiveness and detail.

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:25.715
One pillar is devoted entirely to Saint Trophimus, the first bishop of Arles, traditionally dated to the 1st century AD.

00:14:07.937 --> 00:14:19.926
Walking out into the center of the cloister courtyard — from here you get a full view of all four galleries surrounding you, a rare moment of calm in the middle of a busy city.

00:14:27.252 --> 00:14:39.247
The open courtyard would once have been used for quiet meditation and study by the canons who lived and worked in the monastery.

00:15:18.299 --> 00:15:30.291
We're exiting the cloister now and heading back through the Palais de l'Archevêché to the main square.

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:38.771
This marble statue is "L'Aveugle et le Paralytique" — The Blind Man and the Paralytic — by the Arles-born sculptor Jean Turcan, who lived from 1846 to 1895.

00:16:41.483 --> 00:16:53.474
It depicts a parable in which two disabled men help each other — one who cannot walk carries the other, who cannot see but guides the way.

00:17:37.768 --> 00:17:49.495
On our left is the small chapel of Sainte-Anne, built in 1614 — it now hosts temporary art and photography exhibitions.

00:18:07.901 --> 00:18:19.892
Facing us now is the façade of the Primatial Basilica of Saint-Trophime, one of the great masterpieces of Provençal Romanesque architecture.

00:18:25.861 --> 00:18:37.862
The portal, carved in the mid-12th century, depicts the Last Judgment — Christ in Majesty at the center, surrounded by the four Evangelists' symbols: the eagle, the lion, the ox, and the angel.

00:18:41.397 --> 00:18:53.399
The frieze below Christ shows the Apostles in a row, while the side panels depict the blessed entering heaven on the left and the damned being led to hell on the right.

00:18:59.357 --> 00:19:11.336
This portal is considered one of the finest examples of Romanesque sculpture in France, comparable to the great portals at Autun, Vézelay, and Moissac.

00:19:33.921 --> 00:19:45.909
The church was built over an earlier 5th-century basilica and reconstructed in its current form in the 12th century.

00:19:56.626 --> 00:20:08.114
Saint-Trophime is dedicated to Trophimus, a bishop believed to have been sent from Rome to Christianize the region, making Arles one of the earliest Christian communities in France.

00:20:22.377 --> 00:20:34.369
The cathedral became a major stop on the Via Tolosana, one of the four great pilgrimage routes across France to Santiago de Compostela in Spain.

00:21:00.001 --> 00:21:11.994
In 1178, Frederick Barbarossa — the Holy Roman Emperor — was crowned King of Arles inside this very church, confirming the city's imperial importance.

00:21:24.738 --> 00:21:36.732
The original 12th-century apse was replaced by a Gothic choir between 1445 and 1465, which you can see at the far end.

00:22:03.163 --> 00:22:15.657
It was raised to the rank of minor basilica by Pope Leo XIII in 1882. Entry to the church itself is free.

00:22:40.099 --> 00:22:52.097
Following the precedent of Frederick Barbarossa, Emperor Charles IV is crowned king of Arles (Arelat) at St. Trophime Cathedral in 1365.

00:23:05.513 --> 00:23:17.503
At the time the cathedral was built, in the late 11th century or early 12th century, Arles was the second-largest city in Provence, with a population of between 15,000 and 20,000 people. 

00:23:31.605 --> 00:23:43.591
It had a busy port on the Rhône, and two new cities, on either side of the old Roman town, surrounded by a wall.

00:23:57.019 --> 00:24:09.863
Arles sits on the Rhône River in the Provence region of southern France — about 55 miles northwest of Marseille, right where the river splits into its delta

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:35.769
The Greeks arrived here in the 6th century BC and called the settlement Theline, attracted by the river's natural crossing point.

00:24:49.204 --> 00:25:00.692
The Celtic-Ligurian people later renamed it Arelate, meaning "place by the marshes" — the root of the modern name Arles.

00:25:13.941 --> 00:25:25.929
In 46 BC, Julius Caesar founded a Roman colony here for veterans of the Sixth Legion, rewarding the city for its loyalty during the civil war against Pompey.

00:25:54.150 --> 00:26:06.136
The square has looked essentially like this since the 17th century — the obelisk, the fountain, the town hall, the cathedral, and the palace forming a perfect ensemble.

00:26:13.527 --> 00:26:25.524
The Hôtel de Ville — Arles' town hall — was completed in 1676 as a symbol of the city's prosperity under Louis XIV.

00:26:30.131 --> 00:26:42.128
Its grand vaulted entrance hall is considered a masterpiece of 17th-century French stonecutting — the flat ceiling held in place by compression alone, with no iron reinforcement.

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:59.531
The Cryptoportiques are a network of subterranean Roman galleries that formed the foundation of the Roman forum above, built around 25-10 BC under Augustus.

00:28:17.326 --> 00:28:29.316
Down here the air is cool — the galleries form a U-shape approximately 90 meters long, with barrel-vaulted ceilings of Roman brick.

00:28:47.641 --> 00:28:59.639
The purpose of the Cryptoportiques is still debated — they may have served as warehouses, grain storage, or simply as a massive foundation to level the uneven ground for the forum above.

00:29:19.293 --> 00:29:31.279
The galleries are among the best-preserved Roman underground structures in France, and were used as a hiding place by the French Resistance during World War II.

00:29:52.162 --> 00:30:05.005
The projections and art installations down here are part of a contemporary display that uses the ancient walls as a canvas — a striking contrast of old and very new.

00:30:21.687 --> 00:30:33.675
The Cryptoportiques can be visited as part of the multi-site pass covering the Roman monuments of Arles — one of the most atmospheric and least-visited sites in the city.

00:30:37.614 --> 00:30:49.612
Part of the gallery is barricaded off due to standing water, so we'll turn around and head back up.

00:31:26.748 --> 00:31:38.738
Arles in the Roman period had a forum, an amphitheatre, a theatre, a hippodrome, thermal baths, and an aqueduct — one of the most complete Roman urban plans still readable in any European city.

00:32:14.657 --> 00:32:26.648
After the fall of the Western Roman Empire in 476 AD, Arles was ruled successively by the Visigoths, the Burgundians, the Ostrogoths, and finally the Franks.

00:32:51.724 --> 00:33:05.245
In the 9th century, Arles became the capital of the Kingdom of Burgundy-Provence — sometimes called the Kingdom of Arles — which lasted until the 12th century.

00:33:54.872 --> 00:34:08.393
Back out into the Place de la République — we are heading now toward the Roman Theatre, just up Rue de la Calade.

00:34:27.741 --> 00:34:39.742
A quick note — this walk was filmed out of order from what you see in the final edit, which is something I often do so I can film each location at the best time of day.

00:34:51.800 --> 00:35:04.643
I used Nîmes as my base for this trip — the train from Nîmes to Arles only takes about 30 minutes, though I ended up taking an Uber, which was actually cheaper than the train ticket.

00:35:26.887 --> 00:35:38.887
Rue de la Calade is one of the oldest streets in Arles, a narrow lane leading from the main square toward the Roman Theatre.

00:36:34.319 --> 00:36:47.162
The Roman Theatre of Arles was built during the reign of Augustus around 12 BC, making it one of the oldest Roman monuments in the city.

00:37:06.510 --> 00:37:18.508
It predates the amphitheatre by about a century and was one of the earliest stone theatres in all of Roman Gaul.

00:38:01.204 --> 00:38:13.186
The theatre originally held around 8,000 spectators and featured a full stage wall — scaenae frons — though most of it was dismantled in the Middle Ages for building stone.

00:38:27.081 --> 00:38:39.074
Walking out onto the orchestra floor — the semicircular inlaid marble pattern marks where the orchestra pit once was, directly in front of the raised stage.

00:38:52.494 --> 00:39:04.484
The famous Venus of Arles was discovered here in 1651 — a marble statue of Aphrodite now in the Louvre in Paris, with a copy at the Musée de l'Arles Antique.

00:39:37.952 --> 00:39:49.939
The stage area is set up with scaffolding for an upcoming performance — the theatre still hosts concerts and festivals under the open Provençal sky.

00:40:15.693 --> 00:40:27.680
From the seating area you see the full picture — the cavea sweeping around, the two tall surviving columns, and the Saint-Trophime bell tower rising beyond.

00:41:12.959 --> 00:41:24.958
Only two columns from the original stage wall survive — locals have long called them "the Two Widows," the last of over a hundred that once adorned the stage.

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:48.480
From the top tier, you have a view out over the rooftops of Arles — the orange terracotta tiles, the church spires, and the trees of the Jardin d'été below.

00:41:51.870 --> 00:42:04.036
Here is our first view of the amphitheater. 

00:42:04.116 --> 00:42:16.085
Walking around the top of the seating — the back wall of the theatre is a massive section of original Roman masonry still standing to near its original height.

00:42:25.915 --> 00:42:37.906
That tall section of wall is all that survives of a multi-story stage backdrop — imagine it covered in marble veneer, sculpture, and gilded decoration in the Roman period.

00:43:23.181 --> 00:43:35.151
Most of the relics unearthed during excavations are on display at the Musée Départemental Arles Antique. 

00:43:47.918 --> 00:43:59.906
The Arles Roman Theater has been listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1981. 

00:44:13.332 --> 00:44:25.328
It is now both a historical monument open to visitors and a venue for concerts and events, especially during the "Rencontres d'Arles."

00:44:31.428 --> 00:44:43.415
The original spectators entry is at the Jardin d'Eté beside the Boulevard des Lices in Arles. 

00:44:51.421 --> 00:45:04.264
Just beyond the back wall is the Jardin d'été, the Summer Garden — a public park on the former site of the theatre's exterior portico.

00:45:41.233 --> 00:45:53.219
Scattered across this area are hundreds of stone blocks and carved architectural fragments — column capitals, friezes, and cornices recovered from the theatre over the centuries.

00:46:24.394 --> 00:46:36.388
The stage itself is gated off today — likely being set up or torn down for a performance. The theatre hosts a busy program each year, including opera and summer concerts.

00:46:47.775 --> 00:46:59.777
The theatre was quarried heavily for building material throughout the Middle Ages — stone from here ended up in churches, houses, and walls all across the old city.

00:48:20.282 --> 00:48:32.279
We're heading out of the Roman Theatre now and toward the amphitheatre — just a short walk to the north.

00:50:08.376 --> 00:50:20.351
And there it is — the Arènes d'Arles, rising above the surrounding buildings, its two tiers of 60 arches each unmistakable against the blue sky.

00:50:34.066 --> 00:50:46.056
The amphitheatre was built around 90 AD, just a decade or so after the Colosseum in Rome — modeled on it, but slightly smaller and in far better condition.

00:51:05.669 --> 00:51:17.663
It measures 136 meters long by 109 meters wide, with 120 arches arranged over two tiers — 60 Doric arches below, 60 Corinthian above.

00:51:49.152 --> 00:52:01.121
Walking along the street beside the amphitheatre — the wall rises some 21 meters, containing enough stone to have constructed an entire medieval town.

00:52:22.613 --> 00:52:34.612
In Roman times, it held over 20,000 spectators for gladiator games, animal hunts, and public spectacles — all free of charge, paid for by wealthy citizens or the emperor.

00:54:31.038 --> 00:54:43.038
Admission to the amphitheatre is around 10 euros for adults, or you can buy a combination pass covering the theatre and other monuments.

00:55:13.442 --> 00:55:25.430
Passing through the entrance tunnel — the Romans called these vomitoria — each one designed to channel thousands of spectators rapidly to their seats.

00:55:55.456 --> 00:56:08.300
Stepping into the arena for the first time — the scale is breathtaking, a full oval of seating rising all around, the sandy floor ringed with the red barrier used for modern bullfighting.

00:56:24.566 --> 00:56:36.561
Today the arena hosts the Féria de Pâques at Easter and the Féria du Riz in September, making it one of the most active bullfighting venues in France.

00:57:13.309 --> 00:57:25.304
The inner corridors are a remarkable feat of Roman engineering — a system of vaulted passages that allowed 20,000 people to enter and exit the building rapidly and safely.

00:57:48.426 --> 00:58:00.592
Back outside in the seating — the arena floor is sand over a system of underground passages and trapdoors, through which animals and gladiators could appear suddenly in the arena.

00:58:21.295 --> 00:58:33.268
The stones used to build the amphitheatre are local Mesozoic limestone, quarried inland and transported here by river.

00:58:58.230 --> 00:59:10.226
This outer corridor runs the full perimeter just inside the exterior wall — the iron fence opens out to the street, and the inner archways give access to the seating above.

00:59:15.512 --> 00:59:27.513
The directional signs on the wall — Toril Bas, Toril Haut, Centrale — guide performers and staff to different sections during bullfights and concerts.

01:00:23.073 --> 01:00:35.060
From this side of the arena, you get the full panorama — the sweeping curve of the seating, the sandy floor, and the medieval tower rising above the arches at the far end.

01:00:45.439 --> 01:00:57.431
Four medieval watchtowers were added for defense — three of which still stand today. 

01:01:25.761 --> 01:01:37.749
After the Western Roman Empire fell, the amphitheatre was transformed into a fortified village — over 200 houses, two chapels, and a public square were built inside its walls.

01:02:09.813 --> 01:02:21.815
The tower to our left is the bell tower of the Église Couvent des Cordeliers. 

01:02:56.236 --> 01:03:09.079
The seating here retains the original Roman stone rows, worn smooth by two thousand years of use — sit down and you are sitting on the same stone as a spectator in the year 100 AD.

01:04:02.990 --> 01:04:14.988
Walking along the upper corridor — it is easy to imagine this place full of 20,000 spectators, the noise, the heat, the smell of the sand, and the roar of the crowd below.

01:05:01.612 --> 01:05:13.600
This medieval settlement inside the arena lasted for nearly a thousand years — from around the 6th century all the way until 1830.

01:05:35.965 --> 01:05:47.957
Here you can see the new modern seating. 

01:06:27.499 --> 01:06:39.484
In 1825, the writer Prosper Mérimée — France's first inspector of historical monuments — began the process of clearing the arena and restoring it.

01:07:07.145 --> 01:07:19.142
The houses inside were expropriated and demolished, and the first modern bullfight was held in 1830 to celebrate the French conquest of Algiers.

01:07:53.230 --> 01:08:05.396
In 1481, the County of Provence was bequeathed to the French crown, and Arles — along with the rest of Provence — became part of France, where it has remained ever since.

01:08:20.307 --> 01:08:32.307
We are directly below the medieval tower — one of those added during the period when the amphitheatre was used as a fortified settlement.

01:08:35.330 --> 01:08:47.327
Let's go get a view from the top!

01:09:03.679 --> 01:09:15.676
Vincent van Gogh painted this very amphitheatre in 1888 — his painting "Les Arènes" shows the crowd gathered here for a bullfight, a tradition that continues today.

01:09:43.325 --> 01:09:55.324
Looking down from here, you get a great view of the steps and the piazza in front of the amphitheatre, with the green umbrellas of Brasserie L'Aficion below.

01:10:21.391 --> 01:10:33.382
Inside the hollow tower, the stone construction is rough and simple compared to the Roman arches below — medieval builders using the amphitheatre's own materials.

01:10:49.855 --> 01:11:02.021
From the top of the tower, the entire arena opens up below — sand, red barrier, seating rows curving all around, and the rooftops of Arles stretching out beyond.

01:11:06.388 --> 01:11:18.374
From this viewpoint, you are looking out over the full oval of the arena — in ancient times, an awning called a velarium could be stretched over part of the seating to shade spectators.

01:11:44.001 --> 01:11:55.999
Looking in the other direction, you can see Rue Voltaire stretching away toward the Porte de la Cavalerie, with the Rhône visible in the distance.

01:12:10.232 --> 01:12:22.221
One more look at the full arena from up here — it is remarkable how complete this building is after nearly 2,000 years, still serving its original purpose as a venue for public spectacles.

01:12:34.969 --> 01:12:46.959
On this side of the arena, the original Roman stone seating has been supplemented with modern metal scaffolding-style benches to increase capacity for modern events.

01:13:02.417 --> 01:13:14.415
The amphitheatre can seat up to 12,000 people in its current configuration — far fewer than the original 20,000, but the major feria events still sell out quickly.

01:14:21.370 --> 01:14:33.364
Under the metal seating framework, you can still see the original Roman stone rows preserved beneath — two layers of history visible in one glance.

01:16:04.720 --> 01:16:16.702
Walking through the stone corridor under the seating — the cool vaulted passages are original Roman construction, giving a sense of what it was like to move through this building two millennia ago.

01:18:08.660 --> 01:18:20.661
Back out in the seating at the lower level — looking across at the box seat at the far end of the arena, the most privileged position in the entire amphitheatre.

01:18:37.728 --> 01:18:49.717
This is the box seat — a reserved enclosure at arena level, equipped with its own small benches facing the action.

01:18:50.263 --> 01:19:03.106
Let's sit down here for a moment and see what it would be like to witness a bull fight from this position. 

01:19:48.207 --> 01:20:00.373
Walking along the bottom row now — the seats are all numbered, a modern system for managing seating during the bullfighting events that still take place here.

01:20:17.688 --> 01:20:29.687
The Romans assigned seating strictly by social class — senators and magistrates at the front, ordinary citizens in the middle, women and slaves at the top.

01:20:57.715 --> 01:21:08.525
Here are seats 1 through 30. 

01:21:17.665 --> 01:21:28.463
Camargue-style bullfighting, called course camarguaise, is bloodless — bullfighters try to remove ribbons from the bull's horns rather than harm the animal.

01:22:35.778 --> 01:22:47.765
Descending the steps at the front of the amphitheatre — at the bottom, Brasserie L'Aficion with its green umbrellas is one of the most popular spots for a post-visit lunch.

01:23:13.519 --> 01:23:25.506
Turning back to look up at the amphitheatre from street level — the building is even more impressive from down here, the full height of the two-story façade rising up against the sky.

01:23:55.198 --> 01:24:08.041
Now walking down Rue Voltaire toward the Porte de la Cavalerie — the medieval northern gate of the old town.

01:24:31.084 --> 01:24:43.072
Many of the buildings along Rue Voltaire are draped in green vines — a classic detail of Provençal townscapes that gives even ordinary streets a beautiful quality in the summer.

01:25:09.375 --> 01:25:21.363
This triangular piazza at the intersection of Rue Voltaire and Rue Augustin Tardieu is a lively spot with café seating spilling out onto the cobblestones.

01:26:03.253 --> 01:26:15.251
Arles draws visitors from all over the world — in ancient times it was a crossroads of the Roman Mediterranean, and that cosmopolitan character has never really left.

01:27:17.462 --> 01:27:29.456
We'll stop to look at this fountain on our left on the way back — the Fontaine Amédée Pichot, one of the distinctive landmarks of this street.

01:27:37.455 --> 01:27:49.438
Up ahead is the Porte de la Cavalerie — the northern gate of the old city, dating back to the 13th century and rebuilt in the 16th and 18th centuries.

01:27:52.827 --> 01:28:04.316
The gate takes its name from the Knights Templar, who were based in this neighborhood during the Middle Ages.

01:28:11.465 --> 01:28:23.467
The two round towers were partially damaged during the French Revolution and again in 1877 — the central gate itself was demolished in the 19th century.

01:28:29.424 --> 01:28:41.421
If you are arriving by train from Nîmes — just 30 minutes away — the station is a short walk from this gate, making it the most natural starting point for visitors coming by rail.

01:28:42.098 --> 01:28:54.090
Earlier today, this area was a food market. 

01:28:58.835 --> 01:29:10.827
In fact, I started today by filming the market, but I decided to include the market section of the tour after we toured the town. 

01:29:20.316 --> 01:29:32.315
The flea market starts at time 2:17:47. The clothing market starts at time 2:22:53 and the food market starts at time 2:32:06. 

01:30:05.997 --> 01:30:17.991
Now back at the Amédée Pichot fountain. It was built in 1887 as a tribute to Arles-born writer Amédée Pichot, a historian, novelist, and translator.

01:30:34.800 --> 01:30:46.796
Pichot was the first person to translate Lord Byron and Sir Walter Scott into French — a major figure in 19th-century literary life.

01:31:00.892 --> 01:31:12.868
The central medallion is by local painter Paul Balze, based on Raphael's allegory of Poetry — a tribute both to Pichot and to the friendship between the two men.

01:31:22.918 --> 01:31:34.906
Walking back along Rue Voltaire — one of the main commercial streets of the old town, connecting the Porte de la Cavalerie with the amphitheatre.

01:32:28.445 --> 01:32:40.436
Now turning down Rue du 4 Septembre — a charming narrow cobblestone street with local shops and café tables spilling out onto the pavement.

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:12.166
The street name commemorates September 4, 1870 — the day the French Third Republic was proclaimed in Paris following the defeat of Napoleon III.

01:33:52.829 --> 01:34:05.672
Walking down Rue de l'Amphithéâtre — the amphitheatre fills the end of the street perfectly, a 2,000-year-old monument completely integrated into daily city life.

01:34:24.343 --> 01:34:36.343
The ivy and purple morning glory flowers climbing the buildings along this street are a reminder of how lush Provence becomes in the warm months.

01:34:52.468 --> 01:35:05.989
And we're back at the green umbrellas and the steps leading up to the amphitheatre — we'll walk up and around the opposite side this time.

01:35:40.247 --> 01:35:52.242
Walking alongside the amphitheatre on this opposite side — the street curves along the exterior wall, and you feel the sheer scale of the building.

01:36:09.728 --> 01:36:21.715
This neighborhood around the amphitheatre has been a center of commerce and hospitality since Roman times — inns and food stalls have always gathered outside great Roman venues.

01:38:05.404 --> 01:38:17.405
This is Notre-Dame-de-la-Major, a church perched on the highest point in the old town, with foundations going back to at least the 5th century.

01:38:45.050 --> 01:38:57.047
The current structure dates largely from the 12th century, rebuilt on the site of a Roman temple — its bell tower and façade were added in the 17th century.

01:39:23.341 --> 01:39:35.338
Inside, the church is simple and serene — stone walls, wooden pews, and the red-and-yellow Provençal flag hanging from the columns, honoring the regional identity.

01:40:07.731 --> 01:40:19.730
Notre-Dame-de-la-Major is the spiritual home of the Gardians — the brotherhood of Camargue cowboys, founded in 1512.

01:40:41.278 --> 01:40:53.270
Every May 1st, the Gardians ride their horses here from the Place de la République for a blessing — one of the oldest living traditions in Provence.

01:41:19.569 --> 01:41:31.562
The church was heavily damaged during the Allied bombing raids of August 1944, then carefully restored over the following decades.

01:42:13.825 --> 01:42:25.823
Look to our right. We will see the bottom of these steps later in the tour while walking through the market. 

01:42:46.085 --> 01:42:58.075
From the panoramic viewpoint near the church, you get a stunning look out over the terracotta rooftops of Arles — a sea of orange tiles stretching to the horizon.

01:43:20.649 --> 01:43:32.636
This view captures why Arles was so beloved by artists — the quality of the Provençal light, the warm stone, and the density of the old town create a landscape unlike anywhere else.

01:43:45.386 --> 01:43:57.375
The parking lot beside Notre-Dame-de-la-Major is one of the most convenient spots for visiting the amphitheatre — a short walk to all the major monuments.

01:44:26.388 --> 01:44:38.383
Arles is a very walkable city — the historic center is compact and most of the major monuments are within 10 to 15 minutes of each other on foot.

01:45:00.001 --> 01:45:11.990
The city is best visited in spring or autumn — September is ideal, with warm temperatures, manageable crowds, and the harvest season beginning in the vineyards.

01:45:52.809 --> 01:46:04.785
Vincent van Gogh arrived in Arles in February 1888 and spent 15 months here, producing over 300 paintings and drawings inspired by the Provençal light.

01:46:29.182 --> 01:46:39.714
Van Gogh famously cut off part of his ear here in December 1888, following a confrontation with Paul Gauguin, who had come to Arles at Van Gogh's invitation.

01:47:02.367 --> 01:47:14.365
Walking back around the other side of the amphitheatre — a completely different feel from the shaded side earlier, with the full afternoon sun lighting up the ancient stone.

01:47:30.292 --> 01:47:42.287
The UNESCO designation covers eight monuments in Arles, including the amphitheatre, the theatre, the cryptoportiques, the baths, the cloister, and the Alyscamps necropolis.

01:47:55.706 --> 01:48:07.708
The color of the limestone changes dramatically depending on the light — in the morning it is a cool grey, and in the afternoon sun it turns the warm golden yellow that defines Provence.

01:48:27.558 --> 01:48:39.549
Back at the front steps of the amphitheatre — a nice side view this time with people walking up and down and the green umbrellas of L'Aficion in the background.

01:49:53.966 --> 01:50:06.132
We are now walking back down Rue de l'Amphithéâtre toward the river.

01:50:29.886 --> 01:50:41.874
Arles has a strong culinary tradition rooted in Provençal and Camargue cooking — tapenade, anchoïade, gardiane de taureau, and locally grown Camargue rice.

01:51:18.471 --> 01:51:30.467
This is the Église Saint-Julien d'Arles — the original church on this site was consecrated in the 12th century by Pope Callixtus II.

01:51:46.733 --> 01:51:58.723
The current building was reconstructed in 1662 in a late Gothic style — taller, larger, and more ornate than its predecessor.

01:52:10.792 --> 01:52:22.778
Inside, the pointed arches and vaulted ceiling are classic southern French Gothic — a notable Baroque altarpiece and pipe organ remain from the original furnishings.

01:52:36.206 --> 01:52:48.200
The church suffered heavy damage during the Allied bombings of August 1944 and was rebuilt in the 1950s — it now hosts concerts and exhibitions.

01:53:41.605 --> 01:53:53.596
The streets in this part of the old town are among the quietest and most charming in Arles — narrow, shaded, and largely free of tourist traffic even in the busy season.

01:56:28.658 --> 01:56:40.647
We are heading down to the bank of the Rhône — the river that made Arles one of the great trading cities of the ancient world.

01:57:00.001 --> 01:57:11.987
The Rhône is the fourth-longest river in France, rising in the Swiss Alps and flowing over 800 kilometers before splitting into two branches here at Arles.

01:57:33.210 --> 01:57:45.210
In Roman times, the docks of Arles were among the busiest in the western Mediterranean — wine, olive oil, grain, and manufactured goods all passed through here.

01:58:06.757 --> 01:58:18.751
Down the steps from the river walkway now — the curved apse of the Baths of Constantine rises just above the buildings ahead.

01:59:07.412 --> 01:59:19.413
These are the Thermes de Constantin — the Baths of Constantine — the best-preserved Roman baths in France, built in the 4th century AD.

01:59:44.489 --> 01:59:56.487
Roman baths were not just places to wash — they were social centers where citizens gathered to exercise, conduct business, read, and socialize for hours at a time.

02:00:20.069 --> 02:00:32.061
A large bath complex like this included a frigidarium (cold room), a tepidarium (warm room), and a caldarium (hot room), each maintained at different temperatures by the hypocaust below.

02:00:54.294 --> 02:01:07.137
Constantine spent a good deal of time in Arles — the city served as one of his imperial residences and was one of the most important in the western empire.

02:01:18.353 --> 02:01:30.340
These stacked brick columns beneath the floor are the pilae of the hypocaust system — hot air from a furnace was pumped through the gaps to heat the floor and walls above.

02:01:50.544 --> 02:02:04.743
The Romans were among the first to develop underfloor heating on a large scale — the hypocaust system was used across the empire from Britain to Syria.

02:02:22.735 --> 02:02:34.736
The tiles used to build the pilae are thin and precisely stacked — maintaining even heat distribution across a large room required considerable engineering skill.

02:02:55.604 --> 02:03:08.447
The baths were built right on the bank of the Rhône, which provided the enormous quantities of water needed to fill the pools and operate the bathing system.

02:03:29.829 --> 02:03:41.817
The massive semicircular apse visible from outside is the caldarium — the hot room — its brick vaulting still impressively intact after 1,700 years.

02:04:04.054 --> 02:04:16.040
Entry to the Baths of Constantine is about 4 euros, or included in the Arles monument pass.

02:04:34.339 --> 02:04:46.330
Turning left now onto Rue Truchet — heading toward the Place du Forum, the liveliest square in the old city.

02:05:05.175 --> 02:05:17.169
These narrow streets between the baths and the Forum give a good sense of the medieval city that grew up within the Roman one — the street layout preserving ancient routes.

02:06:03.797 --> 02:06:15.798
This is the Église des Frères Prêcheurs — originally a 14th-century Dominican church, one of the oldest Gothic churches in the region.

02:06:29.211 --> 02:06:41.203
Today the space is part of the Rencontres d'Arles — the city's internationally renowned annual photography festival, held every summer since 1970.

02:06:56.659 --> 02:07:09.502
The Rencontres is one of the most prestigious photography festivals in the world, drawing tens of thousands of visitors each July.

02:08:14.256 --> 02:08:26.258
This is the Place du Forum — the liveliest square in Arles, shaded by tall plane trees and surrounded by restaurant terraces on all sides.

02:08:52.547 --> 02:09:05.390
The square takes its name from the Roman forum that once occupied this area — two columns from the forum portico are still preserved in the façade of a hotel just off the square.

02:09:26.094 --> 02:09:38.089
Van Gogh painted this very square in 1888 — the famous "Café Terrace at Night," inspired by a café that stood right here on the Place du Forum.

02:09:51.509 --> 02:10:03.505
The Place du Forum is the social heart of modern Arles — where locals come for coffee in the morning, lunch in the afternoon, and a glass of Provençal rosé in the evening.

02:10:14.212 --> 02:10:26.199
This statue is of Frédéric Mistral — the Provençal poet who won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1904, and used the prize money to found the Museon Arlaten here in Arles.

02:10:40.304 --> 02:10:52.305
Mistral championed the Provençal language and helped found the Félibrige literary movement — he remains one of the most beloved figures in the cultural history of southern France.

02:11:00.975 --> 02:11:12.975
Up ahead is the Plan de la Cour — a small courtyard that has been the center of civic power in Arles since the Middle Ages.

02:11:26.389 --> 02:11:38.381
To the left of the entrance is the Bench of Justice — a stone step where the Count's officers would sit to take their oaths and swear to uphold the city's privileges.

02:11:47.737 --> 02:11:59.718
The oldest building here is the Palais des Podestats — a 13th-century Romanesque palace that once housed the podestat, the chief magistrate who governed the city.

02:12:05.697 --> 02:12:17.689
These heavy wooden doors with iron bolts are the old prison cells of the palace.

02:12:21.756 --> 02:12:33.742
The plaque above them reads: "Here were incarcerated the Arlesians before their deportation to the Nazi death camps."

02:13:17.667 --> 02:13:29.653
Walking through the town hall vestibule now — the flat vaulted ceiling has been described as the masterpiece of French stereotomy, held in place by compression alone.

02:13:39.693 --> 02:13:51.789
Out into the Place de la République — a perfect transition from the ancient and medieval city back to the grand 17th-century square where we began.

02:14:02.397 --> 02:14:14.396
The fountain at the base of the obelisk has bronze lions and mascaron face spouts — people sit around the edge in the shade on a warm September afternoon.

02:15:43.714 --> 02:15:55.715
We are walking back out to the Boulevard des Lices — the main boulevard that marks the southern edge of the old town and the setting for the famous weekly market.

02:16:18.423 --> 02:16:30.424
We're about to switch back to earlier in the day when I filmed the market — I filmed the market first starting at 9:55 AM, before the main walk.

02:16:45.871 --> 02:16:57.863
Again, we start with the flea market, and outdoor garage sale. 

02:17:53.979 --> 02:18:05.467
The market starts here at the flea market end — the brocante — where vendors spread out second-hand goods, collectibles, vintage postcards, and antiques.

02:18:20.749 --> 02:18:32.749
The time is currently about 10:00 AM. 

02:18:54.974 --> 02:19:06.462
The brocante is popular with both locals and tourists — you never quite know what you will find, which is part of the appeal.

02:19:30.554 --> 02:19:42.546
Arles has held a market on this boulevard since at least the 18th century — the Wednesday and Saturday tradition is deeply embedded in the rhythm of life here.

02:20:04.779 --> 02:20:16.770
The plane trees lining the Boulevard des Lices were planted in the 19th century — they provide welcome shade in summer and give the market its distinctive character.

02:22:43.023 --> 02:22:55.002
Looking across the street is the Tour des Mourgues — a medieval tower built as part of the city's defensive walls.

02:23:17.925 --> 02:23:29.926
Now crossing the street — this is where the clothing and housewares market begins, with racks of clothes and Provençal linens at very local prices.

02:23:45.514 --> 02:23:57.680
The Wednesday market is primarily a local market — the vendors and most of the shoppers are from the region, giving it a genuine, working character.

02:24:00.001 --> 02:24:11.985
These stone steps lead up to the ramparts — one of the access points for walking along the top of the old city walls, with views over the rooftops.

02:24:25.754 --> 02:24:37.757
The rampart walls on the left were built and rebuilt from the late Roman period through the Middle Ages, enclosing the city on its southern side.

02:25:01.756 --> 02:25:13.747
This gated entrance set into the rock is part of the Aqueduc Gallo-Romain — a Roman aqueduct that carried fresh water to the city from the hills of the Alpilles.

02:25:35.773 --> 02:25:47.763
The Roman aqueduct system maintained a precise gradient over many kilometers to deliver water by gravity alone, without any pumping — an impressive feat of ancient engineering.

02:25:58.817 --> 02:26:10.809
This monument reads "1940 — Aux morts de la Résistance" — a memorial to the people of Arles who died fighting the German occupation during World War II.

02:26:30.331 --> 02:26:42.332
Arles was occupied by German forces from November 1942 until August 1944 — the local Resistance network was active throughout, operating in these streets and the surrounding Camargue.

02:27:04.556 --> 02:27:16.556
The rampart walls alongside the market are a constant presence — limestone blocks worn smooth and dark with centuries of exposure.

02:28:51.295 --> 02:29:04.816
From here, you have a beautiful view of the ramparts with the distinctive pointed spire of Notre-Dame-de-la-Major rising above them against the blue sky.

02:29:28.230 --> 02:29:40.202
The market stretches all the way to the Porte de la Cavalerie — on a busy Wednesday morning it is packed with shoppers and the aromas of Provençal food.

02:30:00.001 --> 02:30:11.991
One of the great pleasures of a French outdoor market is simply walking through and absorbing the sights, sounds, and smells — there is no obligation to buy anything.

02:30:30.837 --> 02:30:42.831
Arles is one of the most rewarding cities in southern France precisely because it is not primarily a tourist city — it has a real, functioning civic life that the market embodies perfectly.

02:32:10.799 --> 02:32:22.785
And here is the food market — this is the part I was most looking forward to, and it did not disappoint — one of the best outdoor food markets I have seen in southern France.

02:32:26.726 --> 02:32:38.721
The bread and pastry stalls come first — baguettes, croissants, pain de campagne, fougasse, and the flat Provençal bread flavored with olive oil and herbes de Provence.

02:33:00.001 --> 02:33:11.995
The melons here are the famous Charentais melons of Provence — small, round, sweet, and intensely fragrant, at their absolute best in September.

02:33:27.447 --> 02:33:39.434
Fresh figs, nectarine jaune, nectarine blanche, pêche plate — the extraordinary variety of stone fruits grown in the Rhône valley.

02:34:33.165 --> 02:34:45.164
Green grapes and tomatoes side by side — September in Provence is the height of the harvest season, and this produce has been picked within the last day or two.

02:34:51.264 --> 02:35:04.107
These big red Cerignola olives — originally from Puglia in southern Italy — are among the largest and meatiest olives you will find at any market in the south of France.

02:35:04.658 --> 02:35:16.654
The Meli Melo and Méditerranéenne olive varieties beside them are local Provençal olives, marinated in herbs, garlic, and regional olive oil.

02:35:32.114 --> 02:35:44.109
The olive is one of the defining flavors of Provence — the region produces its own AOC-protected olive oil, most famously from the Vallée des Baux, just 30 kilometers from here.

02:36:12.572 --> 02:36:24.551
Watching the crowd move through the market — a wonderfully mixed scene of locals doing their weekly shopping, tourists exploring, and vendors calling out their specialties.

02:38:50.258 --> 02:39:03.101
Lavender honey is a quintessential product of Provence — the pale gold Miel de Lavande harvested from hives placed among the lavender fields each July.

02:39:19.739 --> 02:39:31.739
A paella stand — this enormous pan holds saffron rice, shrimp, chicken, and vegetables, all cooked together over an open fire. A fixture at large Provençal markets.

02:39:43.261 --> 02:39:55.261
The wine stand across from the paella is selling local Provençal wine — the region produces some of France's most beloved rosés, along with excellent reds.

02:40:48.660 --> 02:41:01.503
The meat stalls offer charcuterie and specialty items — including Camargue bull sausage, a local specialty unique to this part of France.

02:43:05.217 --> 02:43:17.202
A second paella stand — the smell of saffron and seafood cooking over an open flame is one of the great sensory experiences of a Wednesday market in Arles.

02:43:36.731 --> 02:43:48.725
The cheese and charcuterie stands offer the best of Provençal and French produce — aged wheels, soft chèvres, and cured meats made with local herbs.

02:44:18.410 --> 02:44:30.400
The market opens up into a wider space here — this is the heart of the food market, where the best vendors and the biggest crowds come together.

02:44:49.924 --> 02:45:02.767
The cheese stand with its big colorful rolls is hard to miss — red wax, multicolored herb coatings, and purple rinds indicating different varieties and stages of aging.

02:45:53.967 --> 02:46:06.810
Fresh baguettes at the market cost around 1 to 1.50 euros — baked the same morning and sold out before noon, far better than anything in a supermarket.

02:46:30.902 --> 02:46:42.894
In France, bread is bought fresh every day — the tradition of the daily baguette is deeply embedded in French culture, and in Arles there is always a boulangerie nearby.

02:48:40.005 --> 02:48:52.004
The charcuterie stall with salami, porchetta, and filet mignon is sourcing from local producers — the Camargue has a strong tradition of cured and smoked meats.

02:49:18.435 --> 02:49:30.428
This spice stand is one of the most impressive at the market — dozens of varieties from around the world, from Kampot pepper from Cambodia to Malabar from India.

02:49:35.851 --> 02:49:47.848
The labels include Rouille, Paprika, Tajine, and various blends — a reflection of the North African and French culinary traditions that have merged in southern France.

02:49:53.271 --> 02:50:05.437
Most of these spices are about 5 euros for 50 grams — a great souvenir to take home, and they pack well in a suitcase.

02:52:05.084 --> 02:52:17.079
This olive stand has large metal bowls labeled "déguster, partager, savourer" — to taste, to share, to savor — the spirit of a Provençal market in three words.

02:52:42.019 --> 02:52:54.020
The fish vendor is Saint-Louis Poissonnerie from Saint-Martin-de-Crau, just 15 kilometers away — fresh salmon, whole fish, langoustines, and shrimp on ice.

02:53:16.244 --> 02:53:28.243
Fresh fish at a Provençal market reflects the proximity to the Mediterranean — Arles is only 40 kilometers from the coast, and daily fish deliveries are the norm.

02:59:02.243 --> 02:59:14.231
And we're back at the Porte de la Cavalerie — about 12 minutes left in the walk from here.

02:59:37.274 --> 02:59:49.271
The gateway is better appreciated from this outer side — the two round medieval towers clearly visible, flanking where the original gate passage once stood.

03:00:07.570 --> 03:00:19.561
My initial plan was to start this walk with with the market and then entire the old town through this gate and walk up to the amphitheater. 

03:00:28.241 --> 03:00:40.232
The market ended up taking a long time to get through so it would seem odd to start a tour of an old Roman town with 30 minutes of a market first. 

03:00:55.351 --> 03:01:08.194
The time is currently about 12:30 PM. 

03:01:49.905 --> 03:02:02.748
Back at Place Voltaire — a lively pedestrian square with restaurants and cafés at the heart of the neighborhood around the amphitheatre.

03:03:47.826 --> 03:03:59.822
Walking back down the green vine-covered Rue Voltaire toward the amphitheatre in the bright afternoon sun — the light gives this street a completely different character now.

03:04:47.803 --> 03:04:59.785
The amphitheatre comes back into view ahead — walking toward it down this street, you understand why the Romans chose this hilltop location, visible from all directions.

03:05:20.866 --> 03:05:32.858
The green umbrellas of Brasserie L’Aficion are a great spot to sit and soak in the view of the Roman arches after a long day of walking.

03:05:47.974 --> 03:05:59.964
Back at the steps of the amphitheatre — the afternoon crowds are here now, people sitting with ice cream and coffee in the shadow of a 2,000-year-old building.

03:06:31.347 --> 03:06:43.339
The steps in front of the amphitheatre are one of the great gathering places of Arles — a natural stage set where the ancient building provides a backdrop to modern life.

03:07:01.843 --> 03:07:13.845
If you want to visit the amphitheatre, it opens at 9 AM and closes at 7 PM in summer — arriving early gives you the best light and the smallest crowds.

03:07:45.894 --> 03:07:57.888
Out here on Rdpt des Arènes in the afternoon sun — people at outdoor cafés, souvenir stands doing brisk business, and the amphitheatre rising above it all in the brilliant Provençal light.

03:08:20.457 --> 03:08:32.445
The souvenir stalls sell everything from postcards to Camargue cowboy hats, lavender sachets, and reproductions of Van Gogh's Arles paintings.

03:09:00.001 --> 03:09:12.003
Arles hosts several major festivals throughout the year — the Rencontres Photographiques in July, the Féria de Pâques at Easter, and the Féria du Riz in September.

03:09:52.624 --> 03:10:04.790
Thank you for joining me in Arles — if you enjoyed this walk, please leave a like and subscribe to the channel, and I will see you on the next one. Merci!

03:10:32.946 --> 03:10:44.938
Looking back from the viewing platform — the amphitheatre rising at one end, café life playing out below, and the endless blue sky of the Midi above.

